+### Mechanism (`src/runner.rs`)
+
+- **Stop mirror, now conditional.** The wait loop still reads stop and continue reports with `waitid` calls that
+  never reap (`WNOWAIT`, or no `WEXITED`). A stop is consumed, and heimdall-proto stops **its own PID** with the
+  same signal (SIGSTOP when it is not TSTP/TTIN/TTOU or was inherited as ignored) only if all of these hold:
+  - `parent_sees_stops` holds. The parent is in another process group, as a job-control shell puts each job in
+    its own group. A non-job-control script shares the group and would not see a plain-exec stop either. In
+    addition, heimdall-proto is attached to its session's controlling terminal: one of stdin/stdout/stderr is that
+    terminal, or the terminal's foreground group (queried through `/dev/tty` opened `O_NOCTTY|O_NONBLOCK`, read
+    nothing) is heimdall-proto's or the child's group.
+  - The stop persists for `STOP_SETTLE` (20 ms), or for `STOP_GRACE` (200 ms, round 3c's Q18 grace) when
+    heimdall-proto itself was continued meanwhile. That is the stale-report case after a terminal Ctrl-Z and `fg`.
+
+  Otherwise heimdall-proto just keeps waiting, which is S1. Round 3c used a fixed 200 ms. A stop now reaches the
+  shell sooner unless heimdall-proto was just continued.
+- **SIGCONT pass-through.** SIGCONT is caught (signal-hook iterator plus a flag; its default continue action is
+  unaffected, and `execve` resets the handler in the child). On each SIGCONT the signal thread sends SIGCONT to the
+  child's PID if the child is known to be stopped, has no pending continue or exit report, and has not been reaped
+  (the same `exited` guard as the forwarder, so no recycled PID is signalled). A `fg`/`bg` to the shared group has
+  already continued the child, which then has a pending continue report, so no duplicate SIGCONT reaches it.
+- K6a still holds. heimdall-proto signals only its own PID (stop) and the child's PID (continue), never takes the
+  terminal, and never stops another job member. K16 still holds: nothing else is executed, and `/dev/tty` is only
+  queried.
